sometimes i forget just how good  kingsman the secret service really is  and since its prequel the king's man was  just released i decided to re-watch the  original film and it was fantastic based  on a comic book called the secret  service written by mark miller and  illustrated by dave gibbons kingsman is  an ultra violent and highly stylized  love letter to the james bond films of  the 70s and 80s it's absurd it's  shocking and it's just incredible  kingsman has to be one of the most  bizarre yet still amazing movies  released in the 2010s but not many  people seem to be talking about it if i  had to describe the film with a simple  phrase it would be an absurd masterpiece  welcome back to stream in the galaxy i'm  rocket riley and taryn edgerton and  colin frith's kingsman holds a special  place in the list of some of the best  movies released over the past decade  this film seems to be a collection of  simply brilliant scenes with a  remarkable score and incredible fight  sequences from the bar fight to the  underwater training scene this movie  kept me on the edge of my seat the  entire time the film starts us off with  an awesome introduction scene and song  dire straits money for nothing setting  the tone for the rest of the movie it  brings a light-hearted and comedic tone  to the spy thriller genre while not  sacrificing any of the beloved nostalgic  espionage the movie also gives a great  setup within a pretty short amount of  time eggsy unwin whose late father  secretly worked for a spy organization  lives in south london seemingly headed  for a life behind bars however secret  agent harry hart recognizes eggsy's  potential and recruits him to be a  trainee in kingsman's secret service  meanwhile the villainous richmond  valentine launches a diabolical plan to  solve climate change by instigating a  worldwide killing spree valentine played  by samuel l jackson is actually quite  great with an understandable yet flawed  character motivation this movie plays  heavily upon its absurdity whether it's  through a ninja assistant with swords  for legs world leaders heads exploding  in fireworks samuel l jackson with a  fake lisp drinking vintage wine while  eating mcdonald's or a spy umbrella with  a built-in shotgun this absurdity is  used to perfection playing out in some  amazing action-packed scenes including  the initial bar fight scene where colin  firth explains that manners maketh man  but my absolute favorite scene of the  movie takes place in the kentucky church  let's pause here though i could probably  make an entire video on this one scene  since it's likely one of the most  memorable four and a half minutes of any  spy movie ever released if you'd rather  not re-watch this somewhat disturbing  bloodbath skip to this time  otherwise let's dive into it in the  scene harry kills over 40 people in many  horrific yet brilliant ways while  leonard skinner's freebird plays in the  background these four and a half minutes  of brutal chaos took a total of seven  days to shoot since it involves so many  different moving parts but the film's  director matthew vaughn was able to pull  it off with near perfect pacing and  editing the scene's editing is really  what holds everything together since  there's so much madness happening all at  once the original cut of the scene was  actually over 7 minutes long with no  cutaways but then the producers decided  that there should be several cuts to  other members of the cast so that the  audience could catch their breath  between all the chaotic takes happening  in the scene and these cuts were  actually hidden quite masterfully the  scene is also one of very few to  correctly implement shaky cam the subtle  vibration of the frame makes it seem  more chaotic and exciting while in other  scenes that would have been completely  unnecessary lastly the choreography was  perfect using the church's environment  and all of harry's spy gadgets to their  full potential surprising the audience  in some cases at one point after harry  runs out of ammo he pulls apart his gun  so he's able to stab an assailant with  it and he can also be seen using the  church's incense as a weapon with sparks  flying each time he hits someone  although the scene seems like it might  be completely out of control it actually  took one of the highest levels of  control to pull it off everything went  right with this scene the right director  the right editor the right choreographer  and the right actor colin firth the  leading actors for the film were almost  perfect although it was weird to see  alfred play the part of one of the bad  guys and luke skywalker as a college  professor taryn eggerton's character  eggsy had a pretty good level of  character development throughout the  film changing from a lower class  criminal with no respect for people who  consider themselves gentlemen to a  world-class spy with a greater  appreciation for upper class gentlemen  and an understanding that he may not  know everything kingsman actually has a  similar storyline to the first men in  black film where a young man is  handpicked and taken from his daily life  to become part of an underground  organization that takes on global  threats although the film was somewhat  slow in the second act thoroughly taking  its time to set up the final set of  scenes it pulled it together in the  final act returning to its exceptional  pacing lastly i want to talk about the  film's score one which refreshes sounds  reminiscent of classic spy franchises  like james bond composers henry jackman  of captain america the winter soldier  and matthew margerson of x-men first  class teamed up to score the film and  they achieved exactly what the movie  needed a light-hearted and quirky spy  theme with room for serious  action-packed sequences although it had  just a couple of flaws kingsman is still  an absolutely absurd masterpiece if you  enjoyed this video and want to watch  another movie review check out my video  on spiderman no way home also i post a  new video every week so don't forget to  subscribe like this video and thanks for  watching  [Music]  you
 

